{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210730,"nonce":"I68mBIBHlSRUEr0Tt/Pmj39Bzkk=","title":"Closure upvalue writes appeared frozen: CSE memory-dep tracking + 16-binding branch array truncation","message":"Fixed in commit b74c653. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n\n\n`src/mir/opt/cse.rs` was block-local CSE that skipped caching\nside-effecting instructions but never invalidated cached reads\nwhen one occurred. Two `subscript_get v2[0]` reads with a `call`\nbetween them were merged into a single read.\n\nFix: two-bucket cache. Pure operations (arithmetic, constants)\nmerge freely. `SubscriptGet` lives in a memory cache that gets\nflushed every time an instruction that may write the heap is\nemitted (Call*, SubscriptSet, SetField, SetUpvalue, SetStaticField,\nSetModuleVar). Pure cache untouched.\n\n\n`src/runtime/vm_interp.rs` `Op::Branch` and `Op::CondBranch`\nhandlers used a 16-slot stack array for parallel-assign bindings\nwith a \"switch to heap when argc \u003e 16\" path. The read loop was\n`for item in bindings.iter_mut().take(argc)` — which only iterated\n`min(16, argc)` times even when `use_heap` was true. With 17+\nphi params per branch the symptoms were:\n\n- Only the first 16 `(dst, src)` pairs were read from the bytecode\n- `pc` was advanced by `16 * 4` bytes, not `argc * 4` — the next\n  opcode decode started in the middle of unread binding bytes\n- `heap_bindings` only collected 16 of the `argc` bindings, so the\n  remaining phi destinations never received their incoming value\n\nFix: replace `for item in bindings.iter_mut().take(argc)` with\n`for i in 0..argc` (and `bindings[i] = ...` instead of `*item = ...`)\nso every binding is read and either stashed on the stack\n(`argc ≤ 16`) or pushed to the heap vec. Three call sites:\n`Op::Branch`, `Op::CondBranch` true, `Op::CondBranch` false.\n\n**Symptom in the wild:** any function with 17+ live locals at a\nloop header had its back-edge phis past index 16 silently not\npropagated, so writes inside the loop body looked frozen at the\niteration-0 value (or read as garbage `Object` values). This\nexactly mimicked the \"closure upvalue write didn't propagate\"\nshape, even with no closure present. Triggered the\n@hatch:game `Game.run` resize block firing every frame → 1 depth\ntexture leaked per frame → GB-scale RSS in minutes; the 17th\nphi was `lastReqW` and the comparison `winW != lastReqW` saw\n`lastReqW` as garbage every iteration, taking the reconfigure\npath each frame.\n\n**Minimal repro pre-fix (now passes):**\n\n```wren\nclass G {\n  static run() {\n    var v01 = 1; var v02 = 2; var v03 = 3; var v04 = 4\n    var v05 = 5; var v06 = 6; var v07 = 7; var v08 = 8\n    var v09 = 9; var v10 = 10; var v11 = 11; var v12 = 12\n    var v13 = 13; var v14 = 14; var v15 = 15; var v16 = 16\n    var idx = 0\n    while (idx \u003c 3) { idx = idx + 1 }\n    System.print(\"done idx=%(idx)\")\n  }\n}\nG.run()\n```\nWith 15 prior locals + idx (16 total) → works. With 16 prior +\nidx (17 total) → `Object does not implement '\u003c(_)'` at the\nwhile condition. The 17th block-param binding was the one being\ndropped.\n\nRepro from before the CSE fix is preserved below for posterity.\nThe downstream manifestations (`for-in` body upvalue clobber,\n@hatch:game closure-vs-locals, nested fiber resume) may now fold\nback to these two root causes; revalidate before relying on the\nworkarounds.\n\n\n\n```wren\nclass C {\n  static run() {\n    var n = 0\n    var bump = Fn.new { n = n + 1 }\n    bump.call()\n    System.print(n)   // 1  (correct)\n    bump.call()\n    System.print(n)   // 1  (WRONG — should be 2)\n  }\n}\nC.run()\n```\n\nThe first `bump.call()` mutates `n` from 0 to 1; outer reads 1.\nThe second `bump.call()` runs (and printing inside `bump` shows\n`n=2` while the closure body executes), but outer's read after\nreturns 1, not 2. Inner's writes after the first call are\ndiscarded from the outer's perspective.\n\nAffects every Wren program that uses a stateful closure over an\nouter local (counters, accumulators, callbacks). Was masked\nin earlier hatch-web work because most closures capture\n`this`/`req`/etc. (objects whose mutations go through the heap\nwithout touching upvalue mechanics) — only a handful of\nspec/example sites tripped it.\n\n\n- Reproduces in interpreter mode (the easier mode to debug).\n  Likely the same in tiered.\n- MIR builder DOES box mutated-and-captured locals: confirmed\n  via `WLIFT_TRACE_BOX=1` showing the outer scope's\n  `boxed=[n]`.\n- MIR for the outer body emits `MakeList([n_init])` for `n`,\n  and inner's `n = n + 1` lowers to `subscript_set(box, 0, ...)`.\n- The first call works → the box exists, the closure captures\n  it, the write goes through.\n- After the first call, OUTER reads 1 correctly via\n  `subscript_get(box, 0)`.\n- The second call's prints (inside the closure) read the box\n  and see 1, then write 2 to the box.\n- After the second call returns, OUTER reads the box → gets 1.\n\nEither:\n1. The box's contents are silently reverted when the closure\n   returns, or\n2. Outer's view of the box is reading from a STALE version\n   (different object than inner's box).\n\nThe trace shows inner's mutations on the second call really\nDID happen (\"inside bump: n=2\"), but the change doesn't survive\nreturn to outer.\n\n\n- **Vec realloc moves the data.** `frame.values` is `Vec\u003cValue\u003e`.\n  If a `set_reg` triggered a `resize` between calls, the buffer\n  could've moved — but Vec moves don't reallocate, only growth\n  past capacity does. Most frames pre-size to register count.\n\n- **`std::mem::take` semantics.** The interp's `'fiber_loop`\n  takes `frame.values` into a local at top-of-loop and writes\n  it back at suspend points. This swaps Vec headers but the\n  data buffer pointer is preserved across `take`/`put-back` —\n  so upvalues pointing INTO the buffer should remain valid.\n  But ObjUpvalue.location points to `closed` not into the\n  Vec, so this isn't quite the path.\n\n- **Closure double-creation.** Outer's MIR has ONE\n  `MakeClosure` for `bump`. Both `.call()` sites use the same\n  ValueId. So unless the runtime is re-running MakeClosure on\n  each call (it shouldn't), the same closure is invoked twice.\n\n- **Box reassignment.** Maybe outer's slot for `n` is being\n  overwritten between calls — e.g. some implicit reset. The\n  MIR doesn't show one. Maybe a hidden `Move` somewhere.\n\n\n1. `src/runtime/vm_interp.rs:2780` — Op::MakeClosure path.\n   Confirm `uv.closed = captured_val` snapshots the Box, not\n   the unboxed value. Verify the box pointer is the same\n   across two consecutive MakeClosure runs (if MakeClosure\n   somehow runs twice).\n\n2. `src/mir/bytecode.rs` — emit step for\n   `Instruction::MakeClosure { upvalues }`. The `upvalues`\n   are register indices. Confirm those registers point at\n   the BOX (a List), not the inner numeric value.\n\n3. `dispatch_closure_bc_inner` — pushes a new MirCallFrame\n   for the closure body. Frame's `values` is fresh; closure's\n   upvalues are read via `GetUpvalue` against `closure_ptr`.\n   Confirm `(*closure_ptr).upvalues[i]` keeps the SAME\n   ObjUpvalue across calls.\n\n4. **Add a register-content trace before/after each\n   `bump.call()`** to see what value the box actually holds\n   between calls.\n\n\nHigh. Foundational closure semantics — anyone writing\neven a counter pattern hits this. Memory entries\n`project_nested_fiber_resume.md` is actually a downstream\nmanifestation of this same bug (the fiber test that fails\nrelies on closure-mutated upvalues).\n\n\nUse module-level vars (which use `SetModuleVar`/`GetModuleVar`\nopcodes that bypass the upvalue mechanism) or pass mutable\nstate through a List/Map argument so all writes go through\nheap object methods.\n\n\nSame bug surfaces *without* an explicit closure: writing to an\nouter-scope local from inside a `for (e in events) { ... }` body\nsilently fails to propagate. Hit twice in `@hatch:game`:\n\n1. `var lastTime = Clock.mono` outside the main loop, then\n   `lastTime = now` inside a `for-in` over events. Outer\n   `lastTime` retained its *initial* value, so on tick 2\n   `now - lastTime` still subtracted from frame-1's `now`,\n   producing huge `dt` deltas. Manifested as `Right operand\n   must be a number` once `lastTime` decayed to undefined.\n\n2. `var resizeW = 0` outside a `for-in` event drain, then\n   `resizeW = ew` inside. After the loop, `resizeW == 0`\n   regardless — surface never re-configured on resize, so the\n   render stayed at the initial dimensions while the window\n   grew, producing the \"cropped to a corner\" symptom.\n\n**Workaround for in-framework code:** use an indexed `while\n(i \u003c n)` loop instead of `for (e in events)`. Or stash mutable\nloop state on a heap object passed by reference (e.g. fields on\na class instance) — the `for-in` body can mutate object fields\nwithout tripping the upvalue path. We applied the second\nworkaround in `@hatch:game` by moving `lastTime` / `startTime`\nonto `GameState` as fields.","files":null}]}